</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Pronunciation-discriminative reinforcement learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further enhance the model’s ability to distinguish homophones, we introduce a PDRL strategy that leverages perturbed label sampling to create more challenging training scenarios. Specifically, for each training instance, we generate a perturbed transcription <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing the target keyword <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with its homophone distractor <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In parallel, a perturbed pronunciation-guided context <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{C}}_{gpgd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{C}}_{gpgd}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by substituting the entry <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,\mathcal{T}(w),w^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,\mathcal{T}(w),w^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{gpgd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{gpgd}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{\prime},\mathcal{T}(w^{\prime}),w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{\prime},\mathcal{T}(w^{\prime}),w)</annotation></semantics></math>. This data augmentation exposes the model to both original and perturbed context-label pairs, thereby improving its ability to effectively discriminate between homophones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we adopt MWER for biased words as the reinforcement learning objective. Among all sampled hypotheses, those with a lower B-WER than the average receive positive rewards, while those with higher B-WER are penalized (see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related work ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). To promote recognition across different contexts, the overall loss is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:195.4pt;height:9.4pt;vertical-align:-2.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-10.9pt,0.5pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<p id="S3.E2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{PDRL}}(\mathbf{X},{\mathbf{Y}},{\mathbf{C}})=\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{C}_{gpgd})+\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\tilde{\mathbf{Y}},\tilde{\mathbf{C}}_{gpgd})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>PDRL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>b</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>b</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{PDRL}}(\mathbf{X},{\mathbf{Y}},{\mathbf{C}})=\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{C}_{gpgd})+\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\tilde{\mathbf{Y}},\tilde{\mathbf{C}}_{gpgd})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where the biased MWER loss is formulated as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:196.2pt;height:14.9pt;vertical-align:-8.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-17.3pt,1.3pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<p id="S3.E3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E3.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{C})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}\in\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})}P(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}|\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})\left[W_{b}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i},\mathbf{Y})-\overline{W_{b}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>b</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{b}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{C})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}\in\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})}P(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}|\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})\left[W_{b}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i},\mathbf{Y})-\overline{W_{b}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-best hypotheses generated from the input <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and context <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}|\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i}|\mathbf{X},\mathbf{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized likelihood of each hypothesis, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{b}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i},\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{b}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{i},\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> is the B-WER between the hypothesis and the reference, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average B-WER over all sampled hypotheses. In our experiments, we set <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8</annotation></semantics></math>. We also apply the aggregated CE loss <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{PGCL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>PGCL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{PGCL}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a weight of 0.01 to prevent training divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments and Discussion</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data preparation.</span> To validate the effectiveness of our proposed approach, we conduct experiments on both English and Mandarin datasets. For training, we use the 960-hour Librispeech corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> for English and the 170-hour AISHELL-1 corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> for Mandarin to adapt pre-trained LLM-based ASR models with contextual information. For each training utterance, keywords are randomly selected from the reference label, and the context is constructed by combining the keywords with a randomly sampled number of arbitrary words, ranging from 1 to 100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. For evaluation, we adopt the Librispeech test-clean and test-other sets for English, following the artificial biasing list protocol from previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>: the 5000 most frequent words in the Librispeech training set are considered common, while all others are regarded as rare. For Mandarin, we use the publicly available AISHELL-1 test subsets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>: test-small, test-middle, and test-large, which contain 235, 808, and 1,334 utterances, respectively, with corresponding keyword lists of 187, 400, and 600 words.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR models.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Our code and models will be released upon publication.</span></span></span></span></span> We employ the open-source FireRed-LLM model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> as our pre-trained LLM-based ASR backbone, which contains 7 billion parameters and demonstrates outstanding performance on both English and Mandarin speech recognition tasks. Further architectural details can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Note that our method can be readily applied to other LLM-based ASR models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training and decoding.</span> All models are trained on 8 NVIDIA G2 140G GPUs, with each GPU processing 8000 seconds of speech per batch. The encoder and adapter modules are fully trainable, while fine-tuning of the LLM is performed using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, with the same configuration as the pre-trained model. The Adam optimizer is used with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, warmed up over the first 1000 steps. During decoding, beam search with a beam size of 4 is applied. To mitigate hallucination issues caused by long contexts, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and train an independent CTC module from the encoder, which is used for phonetic matching to filter out irrelevant keywords.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare the proposed PAC method with several strong baselines, including BR-ASR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and SeACo-Paraformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, which are recognized as leading contextual ASR models for English and Mandarin. In addition, we implement a contextual adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> based on FireRed-LLM (CFL) for comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation metrics.</span> We evaluate the overall effectiveness of our approach using WER for the English test sets and Character Error Rate (CER) for the Mandarin test sets. To specifically assess keyword recognition performance under contextual biasing, we report B-WER for all keyword-related experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12647v1/figure3.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/288;" width="476" height="288" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Attention scores from the final LLM layer. PAC leverages both graphemic and phonemic context to identify target keywords (red in hypothesis), whereas the grapheme-only context leads to recognition errors (green in hypothesis).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main experiments on English and Mandarin datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Overview of PAC ‣ 3 OUR PROPOSED APPROACH ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Pronunciation-guided context learning ‣ 3 OUR PROPOSED APPROACH ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarize the performance of PAC compared to baselines on both Librispeech and AISHELL-1 test sets under various biasing conditions and keyword list sizes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Across all scenarios, PAC consistently achieves the lowest error rates. Specifically, on the Librispeech test-clean set with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2000</annotation></semantics></math>, our approach reduces the relative B-WER by 31.8% and 23.6% compared to BR-ASR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and CFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, respectively. On AISHELL-1, PAC delivers even greater improvements over SeACo-Paraformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and CFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, reducing the relative B-WER for keywords by 60.5% and 34.7% on the test-small (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=197" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>197</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=197</annotation></semantics></math>) set. These results demonstrate that our method, by explicitly incorporating pronunciation information and leveraging the proposed two-stage training paradigm, can significantly boost keyword recognition under contextual biasing. Furthermore, we observe that the improvements are even more pronounced on Mandarin datasets. We attribute these gains to the greater occurrence of homophones in Mandarin, where pronunciation cues are more critical for distinguishing contextual words.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation study and discussion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance of PGCL.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 Pronunciation-guided context learning ‣ 3 OUR PROPOSED APPROACH ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we evaluate the effectiveness of PGCL by training the model with various context configurations as defined in Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Pronunciation-guided context learning ‣ 3 OUR PROPOSED APPROACH ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The results demonstrate that incorporating the grapheme-phoneme with grapheme distractor loss <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{gpgd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{gpgd}</annotation></semantics></math> yields substantial improvements compared to using only the grapheme loss <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or the grapheme-phoneme loss <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{gp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{gp}</annotation></semantics></math>. These findings indicate that our pronunciation-guided context construction effectively encourages the model to utilize phonemic information, leading to more accurate keyword recognition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance of PDRL.</span> We assess the impact of PDRL by enabling it on top of PGCL (see Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 Pronunciation-guided context learning ‣ 3 OUR PROPOSED APPROACH ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which results in additional error rate reductions across all datasets. Specifically, we observe a relative B-WER reduction of 3% on LibriSpeech test-clean and 14.9% on AISHELL-1 test-large. These results demonstrate that PDRL notably enhances the model’s ability to distinguish homophones and rare keywords. Overall, PAC achieves up to 30.2% and 53.8% relative reductions in WER and CER compared to the pre-trained FireRed-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> on English and Mandarin datasets, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of pronunciation-aware modeling via attention scores.</span> To gain deeper insight into the mechanism of our approach, we visualize the attention scores of the LLM backbone during inference. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Experimental setup ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ PAC: Pronunciation-Aware Contextualized Large Language Model-based Automatic Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, when provided with grapheme-phoneme context, the model assigns higher attention scores to phonemic cues associated with target keywords, especially in challenging homophone scenarios where grapheme-only models fail. This demonstrates that PAC leverages pronunciation information to guide the recognition process, thereby improving both accuracy and robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present PAC, a pronunciation-aware contextualized framework for LLM-based ASR. By explicitly integrating graphemic and phonemic contextual information through a two-stage learning paradigm consisting of PGCL and PDRL, PAC effectively addresses the challenges of raw word recognition and homophone discrimination. Extensive experiments on both English and Mandarin benchmarks demonstrate that PAC consistently outperforms strong baselines, achieving substantial reductions in CER/WER and B-WER, particularly under challenging keyword biasing scenarios. In future work, we plan to extend our approach to multilingual and code-switching ASR tasks and to explore more advanced pronunciation-aware modeling strategies.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
